WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.720
Hair dye has been
practiced for centuries.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:07.440
But development of dyes
in the last century or so

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.860
have included chemicals
that may be carcinogenic,

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:11.880
that may cause cancer.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.180
Watch to see what the
science says about the risk.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:22.500
"Do Hair Dyes Cause Cancer?"

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.560
Since there's typically no way
to reverse hair color loss,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.980
the use of hair colorants
is a mainstay of management,

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.940
a practice documented
as far back as 1500 BCE.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.660
While gray hair may carry
a distinguished look

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.140
and limit women's subjection
to a sexualized gaze,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.440
hair color constitutes
a multibillion-dollar market

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.840
with up to 60 percent
of men and women

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.540
in Western countries choosing
to use coloring products,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.120
many of which to cover gray.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.720
For over a century,
aromatic amines

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.820
have been
the main chemicals used

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.580
in commercial permanent,
oxidative hair dyes.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.280
The primary problem
with these compounds

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.920
is that they can be
potent contact allergens.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.000
Product labels often recommend
a small 48-hour test application

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.200
to the skin before use,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.160
but it can take up to a week
for a skin allergy to flare up.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.580
They also may cause cancer.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.200
Hair products contain
more than 5,000 chemicals,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.760
and so it's hard
to narrow it down,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.480
but the aromatic amines,
such as para-phenylenediamine,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.580
that are often used in hair dyes

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.260
to prevent fading
due to washing,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.440
have been picked out
as a "probable carcinogen."

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.140
Before industry reformulation

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.720
in response to
mandated cancer warning labels,

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.060
9 out of 10 permanent
hair dye products

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.440
were mutagenic,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.720
meaning capable
of causing DNA mutations.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.680
This then appeared to translate
into more than ten times

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.260
the odds of DNA damage
in breast cells

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.000
obtained from
the breast milk of women

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.280
who had used hair dye
in the last six months,

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.620
compared to
not using hair dyes at all.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.220
But does it translate
into more cancer?

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.960
A meta-analysis of 42 studies
found that hairdressers,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.700
particularly those
who held their jobs

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.940
10 or more years, were at
significantly higher risk

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.220
of developing bladder cancer
later in life.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.100
Hairdressers, beauticians,
and barbers

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.840
may also suffer
more lung cancer,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.480
voice box cancer,
and multiple myeloma.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.320
There is some evidence that
hairdressers may smoke more,

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.660
but the researchers
took this into account.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:31.200
They conclude that salons
should be better ventilated,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.180
that gloves should be used.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.340
But how much is that
going to help the person

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.980
whose head
has been soaked in it?

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.740
Based on testing
for radioactively-traced

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.360
hair dye ingredients
in the urine,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.420
experiments suggest that
exposure to hair dye

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.940
may be
several hundred-fold higher

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.940
in hair dye users
than the occupational risk

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:51.900
to the hairdressers.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.860
But thankfully there does not
seem to be an excess risk

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.600
of bladder cancer
among those who just

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.100
use hair dye personally.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.760
Furthermore, major changes
took place in the 1980s

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.380
to make hair dyes safer.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.340
In 1979, the U.S.
Food and Drug Administration

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.980
started requiring
cancer warning labels

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.180
on hair dye products,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.780
leading the industry to start
reformulating to eliminate

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.120
the most carcinogenic
ingredients.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.820
A turning point
in bladder cancer risk

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.200
isn't apparent from the data,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.140
but this may just be a function
of the long latency time

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:27.900
between exposure and malignancy
for bladder cancer,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.880
which can be 30 or 40 years.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.000
If you look at
faster developing cancers,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.420
such as leukemia
or follicular lymphoma,

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.200
there does seem to be
a drop-off in risk after 1980,

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.720
though a 2019 meta-analysis,
looking at

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.120
increased
non-Hodgkin's lymphoma risk

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.860
without consideration
of date range,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.520
concluded "exposure to hair
colorants should be reduced

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.020
as much as possible."

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.200
For prostate cancer,
the doubling of odds

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.780
associated with hair dye
use seems limited

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.880
to pre-1980 exposure,

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.040
and there appears to be
no link to brain tumors

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.360
in either time frame.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.420
As a tragically ironic aside,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.480
while the risk associated with
hair products may have declined

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.360
for white men and women,
the opposite trend

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.520
seems to have occurred
for certain hair products

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.320
targeted towards
African Americans.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.780
The Black Women's Health Study,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.240
which assessed exposure
in the 1990s,

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.460
found no association
between breast cancer

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.920
and the use of hair "relaxers,"

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.380
chemical treatments
used to straighten hair.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.000
But in the early 2000s,

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.980
popular straighteners
switched from

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.620
non-carcinogenic compounds,
like lye,

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.980
to chemical cocktails
containing formaldehyde.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.860
So now,
frequent straightener use

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.420
is associated with

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.760
about a 30 percent higher
breast cancer risk.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.280
Thankfully, there's been
a shift towards embracing

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.980
more natural hair styles in
the African-American community,

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.380
resulting in
the sales of hair relaxers

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.320
falling by 40 percent
in recent years.

